7	3	This is the baseline summary. Other summaries will be based off this one.\r\n\r\nTex Tech has been relatively quiet for the past few months. I sent the following email to Larry on November 17:\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nHey Larry, hope you’re doing OK. I couldn’t remember when you said you would be out of the office. If that’s now, please feel free to disregard and we’ll catch up when you get back.\r\n\r\nI wanted to try to sync up on a few outstanding items.\r\n\r\nServer migrations - just waiting for an outage window\r\nClock reader fixes - those are ready to deploy, just need a time when we can take over the Spectra Court laptop for a half hour (although it would be nice to have it a little longer, or have the option to take it over again, for troubleshooting)\r\nWork order archive (issue with reusing work order numbers) - I’ve been working on this over the past couple of weeks and have an updated version to install, and some things to explain about how it works\r\nTTDS updates from ongoing improvements (including fix for issue with Average on COA) - I think all this is ready to go and just needs a time to deploy (either on old servers or new)\r\nI’m also working on some system documentation that I think will be useful for troubleshooting and disaster recovery\r\n\r\nWould it be helpful to be thinking of a time for me to come out there and finish the server migrations, apply and troubleshoot the clock reader fixes, and apply and start doing the quarterly updates? I think we could probably make three good days out of that stuff, or at least two. Or if it’s better to do it as a series of Teams meetings, I can do that, too.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nHe responded with a friendly "thanks" but nothing else.\r\n\r\nIn the interim, I've been working on some background work on TTDS. I sent this Q4 update at the end of December:\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nGood afternoon, hope you're doing OK. I wanted to share a summary of my ongoing TTDS improvements for Q4 2025.\r\n\r\nTo review, Q3 focused on:\r\n- usability and stability improvements (work order search performance, asynchronous processing improvements)\r\n- code quality and maintainability (null handling, data processing performance, observability)\r\n- modernization (C# 12 upgrade)\r\n\r\nFor Q4, since it seems like TTDS upgrades and migrations might be taking a back seat to other priorities right now, I decided to shift gears from the development work I had planned to do and to focus instead on future-proofing around the edges. Specifically, I've been working on (1) support and recovery documentation and (2) automated functional testing.\r\n\r\nThe support and recovery documentation is in a new TTDS "service repository" that I've created on GitHub. My focus has been primarily on:\r\n- what would a new support person or developer need to know about TTDS to be able to work on it?\r\n- what information would we want to have readily at hand in the event of a disaster, especially one requiring recovery of part or all of the system?\r\n- what information might not be accessible to Tex Tech if I were for any reason not personally available?\r\n\r\nI've completed most of the documents I set out to create. There are still a few stubs out there. I'll revisit those as time permits. Please suggest any revisions, clarifications, or additions that you think might be helpful.\r\n\r\nAutomated functional testing involves creating test scripts using a browser-automation and test-running tool called Playwright. These tests are in a separate testing repository. So far, I've created tests that will:\r\n- log onto TTDS using test credentials\r\n- create a work order using the UI\r\n- create a work order and related items programmatically, trigger MRB creation by submitting a failing observation, and then verify that the MRB has been created as expected\r\n- select an MRB disposition and verify that the UI updates as expected\r\n\r\nFuture tests might include:\r\n- creating an inspection session and inputting data\r\n- inputting lab test results\r\n- working with more detailed MRB functionality\r\n- testing the emissions-related features\r\n\r\nI'm describing these tests as "future-proofing" work for a couple of reasons. One is that they document the intended behavior of the system in a way that's (a) maintained with the code and (b) readily consumable by developers. The other reason is regression: with these tests in place, we can be more confident that unrelated changes to TTDS don't break or destabilize these core features that we're testing.\r\n\r\nTo be clear, my only motive for thinking in terms of future-proofing is to be a responsible and reliable service provider. I'm not planning on going anywhere anytime soon.\r\n\r\nFor Q1, I'm thinking it might be valuable to:\r\n- continue work on automated functional testing\r\n- begin work on adding unit tests directly in the code\r\n- explore ways to make the telemetry we added in Q3 more useful for direct TTDS monitoring\r\n\r\nOf course, I'd be grateful for your feedback on how I'm using this time and whether you're finding it valuable.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nAnd I made this status update on Work Item #68 in January:\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI've made a lot of progress on what's turned out to be a pretty huge initiative. I've created a new `IReadRepository<TObj, TKey, TCrit>` interface and a concrete implementation that does (I think) a pretty slick job of generalizing the core Get and Search patterns. I also added (1) a factory that can be used to return typed versions of that repository, and (2) an in-memory version of all that stuff that accepts an input array for testing.\r\n\r\nNext steps are to migrate the rest of the read operations.\r\n\r\nFrom there, the write operations will require a different approach.\r\n\r\n---\r\n	2026-02-09 17:44:42.046+00	2026-02-09 17:44:42.046+00
8	3	# Weekly Client Summary: Tex Tech\r\n\r\n## Overview\r\nWhile we continue to wait for an appropriate outage window to finalize server migrations and on-site fixes, work has shifted toward strengthening the TTDS codebase. The current focus is on "future-proofing" the system through improved documentation, automated testing, and a significant architectural rework to support unit testing.\r\n\r\n## TTDS Infrastructure & On-Site Maintenance\r\nProgress on several key items remains on hold pending coordination with Larry for an outage window and hardware access:\r\n*   **Server Migrations:** Final deployment is ready once a window is identified.\r\n*   **Clock Reader Fixes:** Ready for deployment; requires access to the Spectra Court laptop.\r\n*   **Work Order Archive:** Updated version is ready for installation.\r\n*   **TTDS Updates:** General improvements (including the COA Average fix) are ready for deployment on either the old or new servers.\r\n\r\n**Blockers:** \r\n*   Scheduling an outage window for server migrations.\r\n*   Access to the Spectra Court laptop for clock reader troubleshooting.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## TTDS Development: Quality & Testing (Work Item #68)\r\n**Project: Rework read operations to support unit testing**\r\n\r\nAs part of the Q1 goal to improve system reliability, I have been refactoring how TTDS handles data to make the system easier to test and maintain. This reduces the risk of "regression" (breaking old features while adding new ones).\r\n\r\n*   **Repository Pattern Implementation:** I have created a new `IReadRepository` interface along with a concrete implementation. This standardizes how the system "gets" and "searches" for data.\r\n*   **In-Memory Testing:** I developed an in-memory version of this repository. This allows us to run tests against the code without needing a live database connection, making the testing process significantly faster and more reliable.\r\n*   **Stored Procedure Optimization:** Added support for "side-effect-free" select stored procedures, ensuring that data retrieval operations do not inadvertently alter the database state.\r\n*   **Controller Updates:** I have begun migrating existing operations to this new pattern, specifically focusing on the `WorkOrdersController`.\r\n\r\n**Business Outcome:** This architectural rework ensures that the core logic of TTDS is isolated and verifiable. By moving toward a system that supports unit testing, we are ensuring that future developers can modify the system with high confidence that they are not introducing new bugs.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Documentation & Automated Testing\r\nFollowing the Q4 focus on "future-proofing," the following resources remain available for use:\r\n*   **Service Repository (GitHub):** A centralized hub containing support and recovery documentation. This is designed to ensure that if I am ever unavailable, another developer or support person has the necessary information to recover or maintain the system.\r\n*   **Automated Functional Testing (Playwright):** We now have automated scripts that simulate user behavior, such as logging in, creating work orders, and verifying MRB (Material Review Board) creation. These tests run against the user interface to ensure core business flows remain functional.	2026-02-16 16:49:38.114+00	2026-02-16 16:49:38.114+00
