COPY public.work_item_documents (id, work_item_id, name, content_type, content, summary, created_at, updated_at) FROM stdin;
1	60	2025-12-08T22:19:55.774Z	text/markdown	I've made good progress on a query to extract this data, based on the sample query and the criterial Holly specified.\r\n\r\nNext steps:\r\n- finish including all the data needed (I think it's just the supervisor info)\r\n- analyze any differences with the sample query\r\n  - static comparison\r\n  - compare results	Data extraction query status update	2025-12-08 22:21:29.734+00	2025-12-08 22:21:29.734+00
2	60	2025-12-11T22:16:57.718Z	text/markdown	I reworked the query this afternoon, adding CTEs for the IEP + service combos and for the qualifying events. The final query now just pulls it all together.\r\n\r\nI need to go back and add the school and district to that.\r\n\r\nNext step is to insert that result into a temp table, then find the most recent school (to get the district) and the most frequent therapist (to decide who to send it to) for each student (+service?).\r\n\r\nThere are partially overlapping service assignment records for the same IEP, with completely identical information. That's inflating our record counts. I added a `distinct` to the main select (after going through and making sure I knew that was the only cause of duplicates) to try to counteract that.\r\n\r\nBut actually since the IEPs are student-specific, and currently the only place where the count matters is figuring out which therapist has the most visits for each student, I might take that `distinct` out. It shouldn't matter if the counts are doubled or tripled for the student, since we're only comparing their own visits.	IEP and service data query refinement	2025-12-11 22:21:24.125+00	2025-12-11 22:21:24.125+00
3	60	2025-12-12T22:23:11.648Z	text/markdown	I think this is probably done but I'll take another look at it next time I work on it.\r\n\r\nI was getting a lot more results than the reference query was. The difference seems to have been that I wasn't joining the event service to the IEP service. Once I did that, the numbers got a lot closer.\r\n\r\nStill need to validate the results, but I think this is very close to the right query.\r\n\r\nThere are also still some TODOs with this, based on the "manual checks" in Holly's criteria.	Query refinement and validation status	2025-12-12 22:25:03.193+00	2025-12-12 22:25:03.193+00
4	60	2025-12-26T22:25:40.073Z	text/markdown	I've started work on writing the PDF generation code using QuestPDF. The layout part seems fairly straightforward, with a few quirks at the bottom of the page.\r\n\r\nAccording to Gemini, my main options for unit testing this are (a) snapshot testing, and (b) DOM-based testing. I'll probably do some of both.\r\n\r\nSomething else I hadn't really thought about until I was thinking about testing is the idea of building some QA into the process. We'll want to be reasonably confident that all the expected forms are being generated, and that the PDFs we're uploading contain the correct information. Need to give some thought to how we can automate that QA as much as possible, but also provide some manual hooks into the process.\r\n\r\nNext steps: continue with the PDF generation.	PDF generation progress, testing approaches, and QA integration	2025-12-26 22:28:01.175+00	2025-12-26 22:28:01.175+00
5	60	2026-01-09T19:54:13.114Z	text/markdown	I've generated a bunch of test cases and test data. Holly and Stephanie provided feedback on the test cases. The query passes all the test cases for the test data. I also ran the query against production and sent the results to Holly, Melissa, and Stephanie to review and provide feedback.	Query testing and feedback collection	2026-01-09 19:55:41.011+00	2026-01-09 19:55:41.011+00
6	19	2025-11-06T22:01:52.353Z	text/markdown	On Nov 5, 2025, at 11:25 AM, Jake Bradford <jakebradford@stellartherapy.com> wrote:\r\n\r\nHey Michael,\r\n\r\nJust wanted to touch base with you on why some of the fields are not being populated into the MedicaidInquiry table. I have attached the spreadsheet where it shows the CarrierName through CoverageEnd doesn't have any data. Is this process supposed to be filling in those fields?\r\n\r\nThanks,\r\nJake Bradford\r\n\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nMy Response:\r\nHey Jake, good question.\r\n\r\nMost of those fields are left over from the first iteration of this process. At some point we should go in and drop CoverageType, CarrierCode, CoverageEffective, and CoverageEnd (or maybe we should actually be using the effective and end, not sure!)\r\n\r\nCarrierName should have some values. Status DONE + no CarrierName means we completed the search but didn’t get a match. If there’s a Medicaid ID, you know we got a response from TennCare.\r\n\r\nI’m a little surprised we don’t have any with CarrierName so far, but I guess it’s possible.\r\n\r\nFor what it's worth, those get mapped in two steps. When we’re reading in the HTML response from TennCare:\r\nBLUECARE -> BLUECARE\r\nTENNCARE SELECT -> TENNCARE SELECT\r\nStarts with WELLPOINT -> WELLPOINT\r\nStarts with UNITEDHEALTHCARE -> UHC TENNCARE\r\n\r\nThose are the values you should see in the MedicaidInquiry table.\r\n\r\nWhen we’re creating the insurance assignments, we derive the insurance ID from the carrier name as:\r\nTENNCARE SELECT -> 1\r\nUHC TENNCARE -> 2\r\nWELLPOINT -> 3\r\nBLUECARE -> 7\r\n[null] -> 10\r\n\r\n\r\nSo it could be that we just haven’t hit any eligible students yet. Or we could be mapping something wrong. Might be worth watching the automation flow as it does its thing to see if there are some valid matches that aren’t making it through for some reason. That HTML request and response were really hard to mock accurately, so there could very well still be some glitches with the way we’re processing that page.	Troubleshooting MedicaidInquiry table field population and carrier name mapping	2025-11-06 22:02:56.484+00	2025-11-06 22:02:56.484+00
7	19	2025-11-12T22:26:38.455Z	text/markdown	It seems that we're not mapping any of the student insurance information. We also need to include the coverage effective and end dates.\r\n\r\nI've created an ad hoc HTML table parser that has special methods for capturing the information we're interested in. I'm working now to retrofit that into the existing code.\r\n\r\nI'm also waiting for Eric to send me the message files from his local app data folder so that I can test and troubleshoot against the actual results we missed.	Developing HTML parser for student insurance data mapping	2025-11-12 22:28:44.773+00	2025-11-12 22:28:44.773+00
8	19	2025-11-13T00:40:40.967Z	text/markdown	Tracking in [PR #23](https://github.com/ClearEyeForStellarTherapy/automation/pull/23)\r\n\r\nI decided to ditch my custom HTML parser. It wasn't dealing with comments and a couple of other weird things.\r\n\r\nThe new approach is to use the HTML Agility Pack to read the HTML tables into a custom table structure,\r\nwhich I can much more easily traverse to find specific elements.\r\n\r\nThe "standard" unit test is passing with this approach, but some of the other cases are still failing. \r\nI think the failures may be related to date/time comparisons.	HTML parsing refactor using Agility Pack, with some failing tests	2025-11-13 00:42:57.989+00	2025-11-13 00:42:57.989+00
9	19	2025-11-06T19:44:34.189Z	text/markdown	***status update***\r\n\r\nDelivered the flow and automation service to Stellar last week.\r\n\r\nThis week, Eric Camp is testing it. Today he let me know that he was having trouble importing it because of a Microsoft outage. I sent him some tips. Haven't heard from him.\r\n\r\nAlso, Jake Bradford is working on setting up SSRS reports so that Stephanie and Holly can monitor the status of the verifications and update as needed.	Status update on project deliverables and testing	2025-11-06 19:44:46.874+00	2025-11-06 19:45:02.732+00
10	120	2026-02-27T15:11:17.491Z	text/markdown	I've done a lot of work on making the SQL scripts more idempotent. The last outstanding thing is the constraints and extended properties.\r\n\r\nI need to take another look at the rest of the scripts. The IIS stuff is already mostly idempotent. I think the .NET auth is as well.	Enhancing idempotency of SQL, IIS, and .NET authentication scripts	2026-02-27 15:12:30.078+00	2026-02-27 15:12:30.078+00
35	122	2026-02-06T20:04:19.630Z	text/markdown	OIDC is working, need to see how it performs over time before committing to running on a server	OIDC performance evaluation status	2026-02-06 20:04:57.071+00	2026-02-06 20:04:57.071+00
