28	7	2025-11-07T20:42:51.482Z	text/markdown	Useful script for duplicating the HighlandApp database:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\n-- *** 1. Configuration Variables (!! CHECK THESE LINES !!) ***\r\nDECLARE @SourceDBName sysname = 'HighlandApp';\r\nDECLARE @NewDBName sysname = 'TTDSTest';\r\nDECLARE @BackupPath nvarchar(260) = '/var/opt/mssql/data/temp_copy_backup.bak';\r\nDECLARE @DataDir nvarchar(260) = '/var/opt/mssql/data/';\r\n\r\n-- Variables to hold the full, concatenated path strings\r\nDECLARE @NewDataFilePath nvarchar(260);\r\nDECLARE @NewLogFilePath nvarchar(260);\r\n\r\nDECLARE @LogicalDataName sysname = @SourceDBName;        -- 'HighlandApp'\r\nDECLARE @LogicalLogName sysname = @SourceDBName + N'_log'; -- 'HighlandApp_log'\r\n\r\n\r\n-- *** 2. Perform the Full Backup to the Temporary File ***\r\nPRINT N'Starting Full Backup of ' + @SourceDBName + N'...';\r\nBACKUP DATABASE @SourceDBName\r\nTO DISK = @BackupPath\r\nWITH INIT,\r\n     COMPRESSION;\r\nPRINT N'Backup Complete to: ' + @BackupPath;\r\n\r\n\r\n-- *** 3. Build New Physical File Paths ***\r\nSET @NewDataFilePath = @DataDir + @NewDBName + '_Data.mdf';\r\nSET @NewLogFilePath  = @DataDir + @NewDBName + '_Log.ldf';\r\n\r\n\r\n-- *** 4. RESTORE DATABASE (The Copy) ***\r\nPRINT N'Starting Restore to create new database: ' + @NewDBName;\r\nPRINT N'Using Logical Data Name: ' + @LogicalDataName;\r\nPRINT N'New Data File Path: ' + @NewDataFilePath;\r\n\r\n-- Use the pre-set Logical Names and pre-concatenated physical paths in the MOVE commands\r\nRESTORE DATABASE @NewDBName\r\nFROM DISK = @BackupPath\r\nWITH MOVE @LogicalDataName TO @NewDataFilePath,\r\n     MOVE @LogicalLogName TO @NewLogFilePath,\r\n     NOUNLOAD,\r\n     REPLACE;\r\n\r\n-- *** 5. Final Status and Cleanup Reminder ***\r\nSELECT N'Database Copy Complete!' AS Status, @NewDBName AS NewDatabase;\r\nSELECT N'*** REMINDER: Manually delete the temporary backup file: ' + @BackupPath + N' ***' AS CleanupNote;\r\n```	SQL script to duplicate a database	2025-11-07 20:43:14.225+00	2025-11-07 20:43:14.225+00
29	7	2025-11-08T00:35:43.071Z	text/markdown	I worked on this for a few hours this afternoon, and thought I had it figured out, but I kept running into foreign key problems. \r\n\r\nThinking about it some more, I finally realized what the problem was.\r\n\r\nI knew about the split increment problem, where the work order for the master roll might be archivable but not the work order for the finish roll, or vice-versa.\r\n\r\nBut there's another problem. Both roll scraps and graded defects are tied to inspection sessions. I had to do that in order to attribute those elements to specific inspectors for reporting.\r\nAnd increments are also tied to inspection sessions. So you can have a situation where a roll scrap or graded defect isn't fully archivable, because it's tied to a session that has an increment tied to it that's not fully archivable.\r\n\r\nThat's a solvable problem for sure, but I don't think it's worth it. I've decided on a different approach:\r\n\r\n1. Select all work orders that are archivable based on the date.\r\n2. Archive those work orders and **all** related entities, whether they're fully archivable or not\r\n3. Rather than delete based on the process-related "permanent temp" tables, delete based on what's in the archive table, but _only_ if there are no unarchived dependent objects\r\n\r\nConsequences:\r\n\r\n- Some work orders will take longer to be fully archived, but in all likelihood it'll just be another quarter\r\n- Some work orders will be archived twice, in two separate batches; they could potentially be different; the later archive will be "the truth"\r\n\r\nI'll rework the proc on my next work day and then test it out again using my local TTDSTest and TTDSArchive databases	Revised approach for archiving work orders and related entities	2025-11-08 00:44:13.944+00	2025-11-08 00:44:13.944+00
30	7	2025-11-06T19:55:35.039Z	text/markdown	***status update***\r\n\r\nI think this is basically waiting on me.\r\n\r\nWe installed it in test a while back. It went mostly OK but then did some unexpected stuff.\r\n\r\nI think I can simplify it and make it work better. The problem is that there's this really complicated flow around increments, because increments belong to both the master roll and finish roll, which can be from different work orders, one of which might be eligible for archive and the other not.\r\n\r\nInitially I was trying to have it in both places; copying it to the archive as soon as one work order or the other was ready for archive, and then removing it from production when both work iders had been archived.\r\n\r\nWhat I've realized, though, is that there's really no need for referential integrity on the archive. So I can just leave the increment in prod until it's all ready to copy over.\r\n\r\nSo basically I just need to make that change, test it as best I can, and then schedule another test.	Status update on archiving increments	2025-11-06 19:59:03.085+00	2025-11-06 19:59:33.039+00
