90	37	2025-11-06T20:48:13.326Z	text/markdown	I wanted to also do kind of a “postmortem” on the project, to document some of the unexpected problems and maybe some lessons learned.\r\n\r\nThe first delay was all on my side. I had the idea of using a tool called Terraform to create and manage the AWS resources. I had used Terraform with good results before, but what I was trying to do here was a little more detailed, and some things didn’t work the way I expected. The biggest thing was AWS System Manager, which runs automated processes on AWS nodes. The idea was to set up a System Manager job that we could trigger anytime we made a change to the code, and automatically update the test system. But it just wouldn’t work, no matter what I tried. So I had to shift my approach to be just a little more manual, but in the end, it was still 98% automated, and the remaining 2% only took a minute or so. It really wasn’t worth the time I spent trying to get to 100%. So that part was annoying, but had nothing to do with Stellar Docs, really.\r\n\r\nThe next big hangup was with managing the JavaScript scripts that Stellar Docs uses for client-side interactivity. We’re using the jQuery library, which was so widespread back in its day that it will probably never fully go away. But the versions we’re using are very old. We use version 1.3.2 (2009) in some places and 1.7.2 (2011) in others. I tried to update to the latest stable version, 3.7 (2023), but that version wasn’t compatible with the version of jQuery UI, 1.7.1 (2009), that we use for the datepicker control. At some point, we should probably find a compatible combination, or choose a different datepicker. Having such an old version of jQuery is a security risk.\r\n\r\nOnce I decided to fall back to the current versions of jQuery, the next issue was with how those scripts are bundled for download to the client. The bundling mechanism that the application was built with isn’t supported by the current version of Visual Studio, even for backward-compatible ASP.NET 4.8 projects, which is the last “framework” version of .NET. I would have had to change too many places to use the newer mechanism, so I decided just to include the script references directly on the page. There’s no big downside to doing it that way, it’s just a little more code.\r\n\r\nI finally got through all those issues, and thought I was home free, but then I hit a major snag with the precompilation step of the publish process.\r\n\r\nASP.NET Web Forms supported two different models: Web Site Project (WSP) and Web Application Project (WAP). WSPs were nice for mostly static sites that maybe just needed a little bit of automation here or there. Their compile-on-demand model was perfectly fine for that type of content. For data-driven or highly interactive applications, WAPs offered a better developer experience because they could be compiled before deployment, making the IDE tooling work better, and providing much better confidence in your changes. I’m sure there was a good reason for creating Stellar Docs as a WSP, but ultimately it has made it harder to get it working anywhere except the Stellar server.\r\n\r\nYou can’t really convert a WSP directly to a WAP. The best option is to create a new WAP and gradually files over. That’s what I did, but I kept running into all kinds of conflicts during precompilation. I was able to work through them gradually, but it was a painful process of untangling dependencies.\r\n\r\nException handling was another issue I ran into. On the one hand, having the exceptionLogger table, and a global default error handler that logs the exception details to that table, is a huge help. The problem is with how we use that feature. In some places, it’s fine. The operation fails, the error is logged, and the application continues to function. In other cases, the error is logged, but it leaves the application in a weird state that it can’t recover from, and there’s no choice but to reboot it. \r\n\r\nIn the worst-case scenario, which I saw several times, errors are caught and the quietly swallowed. That happens a couple of different ways. One is in the application code, in the form of a `try...catch` block where the `catch` section is empty. That explicitly tells the application to ignore any errors in the `try` section and bypass the global error handling. The other big case is in stored procedures. I saw several where a complex transaction was wrapped in a `try...catch`, and the `catch` rolled back the transaction as it should, but then basically just returned a “didn’t work” response to the application, without any details. Those were really hard to troubleshoot because I had to recreate the state of the application at the time of the stored procedure call, and then call the procedure step by step until I saw where it failed.\r\n\r\nThere’s also a lot of complicated JavaScript stuff that happens entirely outside the application handling. That caused me a few headaches as well.\r\n\r\nThe last big area of problems was with how we connect to the database. There are several different models in use:\r\n\r\n- via library classes in StellarLibraryCS, most of which use stored procedures\r\n- stored procedures called directly from the code\r\n- parameterized SQL statements called directly from the code\r\n- concatenated SQL statements called directly from the code\r\n- a handful of Entity Framework calls\r\n\r\nThere are a number of subvarieties within those models, and a number of cases where the models aren’t implemented correctly, or, candidly, aren’t doing what the programmer apparently thought they were doing. Having that many different ways of accessing the data isn’t a *problem* per se, but it definitely makes the code base harder to work with.\r\n\r\nA related issue is the sheer number of stored procedures. I’m quite sure not all of them are used, but I was surprised at how many actually were. Some of the operations within Stellar Docs require a long series of stored procedure calls. It makes it hard to troubleshoot, but it also leads to kind of a “worst of both worlds” situation where you’ve got the added complexity of managing stored procedures outside the main application code, but not really getting the full benefits of using them.\r\n\r\nOne last little quirk that slowed me down in numerous small ways was the extent of “dead code” in the code base. Some it was commented out, but a lot of it was just sort of left in there. Probably whatever used to call it got removed. It doesn’t hurt anything as far as the execution of the program (it’s probably not even included in the final compiled version) but it sure gets in the way when you’re trying to troubleshoot.\r\n\r\nTo be clear this is not an “everything is terrible” kind of rant. Stellar Docs has kept on humming for all these years. It *works* fine, it’s just a little hard to work on. And since I spent so much time on this fix, I thought it would be a shame not to capture any kind of lessons learned and maybe some action items that could improve things.\r\n\r\nLong-term, I do think it’s probably worthwhile to consider moving off ASP.NET Web Forms to a more modern platform. It’s not just that Web Forms is *old*—plenty of old stuff still works fine, and Web Forms will likely continue to work fine for years to come—but  the underlying development paradigm ended up ultimately being rejected by the development community (and Microsoft itself). If it were a Java or PHP application from that era, I would say keep it running. Or a Microsoft ASP.NET MVC application from the early 2010s, same thing. But Web Forms was an evolutionary dead end. You’ll have a harder and harder time finding people to work on it, and tools that support it.\r\n\r\nIn the shorter term, here are some recommendations to consider:\r\n\r\n**Migrate to an ASP.NET Web Forms WAP**\r\n\r\nI’ve already done probably 60-70% of the work for that. It would make everything else you do a lot easier, safer, and more predictable.\r\n\r\n**Upgrade to the latest version of jQuery**\r\n\r\njQuery was *so* ubiquitous back in the day, that there are undoubtedly plenty of exploits that target it. Using such an old version is a real risk. Changing to a newer version may require some other changes, including different UI controls, but it may also give us a good chance to hack away at some of the unused code.\r\n\r\n**Standardize the data access model**\r\n\r\nThis is not as hard as it sounds. I basically did it for the therapy screen as part of my fix. Essentially, you choose a model and then make a whole lot of little, mostly very simple, changes to make everything use that model. Considering the current situation, my recommendation would actually be to *mostly* switch to parameterized SQL queries, retaining the more complex stored procedures. \r\n\r\nParameterized SQL is a lot like stored procedures—same security benefits, for all practical purposes the same performance benefits—but your data logic is all located within (or very close to) your application code. While we’re doing this, we could also combine some of those multi-step stored procedure sequences into either parameterized SQL statements or stored procedures, whichever made the most sense.\r\n\r\n**Sanitize the code base for dead code, bad error handling, and other weirdness**\r\n\r\nNot a wholesale rewrite, but selectively and progressively identifying and correcting any of these situations that made the troubleshooting difficult.	Postmortem of technical issues and recommendations for a legacy ASP.NET application	2025-11-06 20:48:29.496+00	2025-11-06 21:53:07.451+00
