94	75	2026-02-24T18:51:23.319Z	text/markdown	I've been doing some research on using Elixir and Phoenix. \r\n\r\n## Summary of Claude conversation (by Claude):\r\n\r\nEvaluated Elixir/Phoenix/LiveView as an alternative to Go/React for a quality management system targeting small-to-mid manufacturers. Initial concern was that Elixir's small community and hiring pool made it risky, but since this is a solo open-source side project, those concerns are largely moot. The real-time, fault-tolerance, and data integrity requirements of the QMS are actually a strong fit for Elixir/OTP—better in some ways than Go. Phoenix/LiveView reduces frontend complexity compared to a React SPA, and the idiomatic approach would be a modular monolith with Elixir contexts (configuration, event intake, reporting/subscriptions) rather than microservices—simpler to operate and a better fit than a polyglot Go+Phoenix architecture connected via gRPC. Ecto (Postgres, obviously) is a good fit given a preference for explicit, non-magical data handling. Library ecosystem is thinner than Go's but adequate for this use case; PDF/reporting is the one area to watch, with a carve-out to a separate component as a fallback. IDE support (VS Code + ElixirLS) is functional but not first-class. Phoenix LiveDashboard provides strong out-of-the-box observability. Deploys as a standard Linux binary, containerizes cleanly for K8s or Fly.io.\r\nThe main risk isn't the technology—it's maintaining momentum on a project with a history of being deprioritized when client work arrives. Scheduled dedicated time has been identified as the key process change to address that.\r\n\r\n## Summary of Gemini conversation about Postgres (by Gemini):\r\n\r\nTo wrap this up for your future self: we successfully moved your Phoenix development environment from a "serverless" cloud (Supabase) back to your repurposed Ubuntu laptop (192.168.1.155) to avoid IPv6 networking headaches and potential usage costs. The setup now bypasses the complex SSL and pooling requirements of managed hosts, opting for a high-performance local TCP connection that provides lower latency and full control over your hardware.\r\n\r\nTechnically, the "magic" was in configuring Postgres to listen beyond localhost (listen_addresses = '*') and whitelisting your dev machine’s IP in pg_hba.conf. On the Phoenix side, we configured dev.exs and .env to target the laptop via its internal IP on port 5432, ensuring the Postgrex driver is forced to IPv4 (socket_options: [:inet]). Your project is now verified, connected, and ready for mix ecto.migrate.\r\n\r\n## Summary of Gemini conversation about Elixir and Phoenix (by Gemini):\r\n\r\nProject Architecture: The "Modular Monolith" (Go + Elixir)\r\nThe Stack: We are utilizing a "Best of Both Worlds" approach by pairing a Go-based Event Engine with an Elixir/Phoenix Dashboard, connected via gRPC.\r\n\r\nGo (The Engine): Handles the "Industrial" heavy lifting—ingesting high-frequency sensor data, managing low-level hardware communication, and enforcing strict data types. It serves as the single source of truth for the system's state.\r\n\r\nElixir/Phoenix (The Dashboard): Acts as the "Air Traffic Controller." By using Phoenix LiveView, we move the UI state to the server, eliminating the need for a complex React/Redux frontend. This allows for real-time, "socket-first" updates (like defect alerts) with minimal code.\r\n\r\nPostgres (The Memory): A unified database that stores both the raw event logs from the Go engine and the relational user/account data from Phoenix.\r\n\r\nThe Philosophy:\r\nThis architecture is optimized for a solo developer building a mission-critical product. It prioritizes uptime through Erlang’s "self-healing" process model and speed of delivery by leveraging Phoenix’s built-in tools (like phx.gen.auth for industrial PIN/Azure AD login). We are trading the "statelessness" of REST for a persistent, stateful connection that survives flaky factory Wi-Fi and provides a superior developer experience without the "JavaScript fatigue."\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nActually I haven't completely decided about whether to use Phoenix at all, and if so, whether to use it for the whole thing in a modular monolith (there are compelling reasons) or only for the UI and then have Go micro services.	QMS architecture planning with Elixir/Phoenix and Go	2026-02-24 18:57:54.409+00	2026-02-24 18:57:54.409+00
