97	8	2025-11-06T19:46:09.145Z	text/markdown	***Email sent to Larry on 9/25 summarizing 2Q25 improvements and plans for 3Q***\r\n\r\nUser & Stability Improvements\r\nWork Order Search Speed: The Work Order Search process has been fundamentally rewritten to run directly on the database, resulting in significantly faster search results and reduced load on the application server.\r\nNote: The underlying search result caching, an occasional source of confusion, has been removed due to the new speed of the query.\r\nIntelligent Task Cancellation: The system now automatically stops abandoned long-running requests (like background searches) the moment a user navigates away. This prevents wasted server resources and enhances overall system stability by reducing the risk of thread pool exhaustion.\r\n\r\nCode Quality & Maintenance\r\nNullability Refactoring: We have begun integrating advanced language features to make the code safer and more predictable. This process has already fixed several subtle, latent bugs that could have caused crashes or incorrect results.\r\nData Processing Efficiency: Corrected internal inefficiencies to prevent the application from repeatedly recalculating or re-fetching the same data during a single operation, leading to faster execution and lower server load across various features.\r\nEnhanced Visibility (OpenTelemetry): Integrated a new industry-standard tracing system. This will significantly improve our ability to diagnose complex performance and logic issues and lays the groundwork for real-time health monitoring.\r\n\r\nPlatform Modernization\r\nC# 12 Upgrade: Updated the underlying programming language. This is essential maintenance that ensures the application remains modern, secure, and easier to maintain long-term, enabling hundreds of small, valuable improvements to the codebase.\r\n\r\nI’ve also started the process of documenting test cases for each part of the application, to help make sure we don’t break anything with these changes.\r\n\r\nFor the next quarter, I’m planning to focus on:\r\nContinuing work on documenting test cases (make code changes safer)\r\nContinue with and probably finish nullability and cancellation refactoring (performance, stability)\r\nAdd a few selected OpenTelemetry metrics to the codebase, investigate pushing “warning sign" metrics to NinjaOne (stability)\r\nAdd unit tests for a few key processes (make code changes safer)\r\nCreate one or two high-level diagrams and/or documents (issue resolution, business continuity)\r\n\r\nI’m also planning to address some outstanding issues and requests as soon as possible:\r\nRework yardage clock code to use asynchronous (overlapped) reads and writes, to try to improve the stability of the connection\r\nAdd a health check on the yardage clock so we’ll know if it’s not working\r\nRebrand “Cheraw” to “Spectra Court” (and CH to SC)\r\nFix the "Average on COA” typo issue\r\nAddress any outstanding issues with the quarterly TTDS archive; I actually can’t remember where we left off on that\r\n\r\nLet me know if it would be helpful to get together and talk through and/or prioritize any of this stuff. And thanks again for letting me do the ongoing improvements. I think I’ve been able to do some good work that will improve performance, reliability, maintainability, recovery times, and long-term supportability. Of course, if I’m spending more time on this than you’re seeing the value for, please let me know.\r\n\r\nWe can put this release in whenever you feel like would be a good time.	Email summarizing 2Q25 software improvements and 3Q plans	2025-11-06 19:46:18.539+00	2025-11-06 19:46:18.54+00
98	8	2025-11-06T19:46:34.218Z	text/markdown	***Initial notes***\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nReview error logs for frequent errors and warnings, fix those\r\nApply static analysis tools to code base, address any issues\r\nUse performance profiling tools to identify and address any performance bottlenecks or memory usage issues\r\nAdd automated unit tests\r\nGraceful degradation - keep core functions accessible\r\nAbility to run app from a single working server\r\nModernize JavaScript codebase\r\nMonitoring and telemetry\r\nShip warnings and errors to S3\r\nDocument troubleshooting steps for frequent problems\r\n\r\n## Review error logs to identify and address frequent errors and warnings\r\n\r\n### Why Recommended\r\nErrors and warnings in the application logs indicate an unexpected condition that could be causing a poor user experience or possibly affecting application security, stability, or performance\r\n### Detailed Steps\r\nReview available application logs, scanning for ERR and WRN entries\r\nFor each entry, determine the cause of the error or warning\r\nTriage the errors and warnings according to:\r\n - impact on information security\r\n - impact on application stability\r\n - impact on user experience and productivity\r\n Identify options for addressing the error or warning\r\n Address any where the cost to fix is clearly less than the impact\r\n If the issue is nontrivial and the fix might impact users or overall system functionality, consult with Tex Tech\r\n### Success Metrics\r\n#### Baseline\r\nnumber of errors and warnings logged per day over 7-day period\r\n#### Expected\r\n100% reduction in errors and at least 85% reduction in warnings vs baseline\r\n\r\n## Apply static analysis and performance profiling tools\r\n\r\n### Why Recommended\r\nLanguages, runtimes, and profiling tools have improved significantly since 2017. Static analysis tools can recommend code changes to make use of these improvements. Performance profiling tools can locate "hot paths" in application resource usage to guide performance optimizations and can point out problematic memory usage.\r\n### Detailed Steps\r\nUse JetBrains Rider to review suggested code actions\r\nApply trivial fixes, make note of more significant recommendations\r\nUse JetBrains dotTrace to collect detailed performance metrics\r\nUse JetBrains dotMemory to identify any memory usage issues\r\nCreate a prioritized list of improvements\r\nImplement the most impactful improvements\r\nPerform the analysis again after each significant improvement\r\n### Success Metrics\r\n#### Baseline\r\nPerformance metrics (e.g., average request latency for key API endpoints, CPU/memory usage during peak load for Legacy) as measured by dotTrace and dotMemory before implementing any optimizations.\r\n#### Expected\r\n - A measurable improvement (e.g., 5-10%) in the performance of at least one identified "hot path" in the application, as indicated by reduced execution time in dotTrace.\r\n - A noticeable reduction in memory allocations or improved memory utilization in a specific area of concern identified by dotMemory (if any such areas are found).\r\n - Implementation of all "trivial" code fixes suggested by Rider.\r\n - Documentation of any significant code improvement recommendations from Rider that require more substantial effort for potential implementation in future cycles.\r\n\r\n## Improve "failure mode" functionality\r\n### Why Recommended\r\nTTDS is generally stable, but there have been instances where the entire application became unresponsive due to either an application-related problem or an environmental problem that could been isolated to a specific section of the application.\r\n### Detailed Steps\r\nList all significant functional paths through the application\r\nIdentify functional paths most critical to Tex Tech operations\r\nList external services on which the application depends (power, Internet, network, domain, database, etc.)\r\nMap dependencies within application\r\nFor each functional path, determine which services and application dependencies the functional path requires (its "demands")\r\nFor each demand, determine whether:\r\n- there is no reasonable functional mode in the absence of that demand\r\n- an alternative could be substituted for that demand, perhaps with limited features or performance\r\n- an explicit redundancy should be implemented\r\n### Success Metrics\r\nAll functional paths and associated demands documented\r\nTop 10 critical paths demonstrated to be as resilient as feasible given physical plant constraints\r\n## Add automated unit tests\r\n### Why Recommended\r\nAutomated unit tests run within the code base and can be highly effective at ensuring that changes and enhancements (1) work fully as intended and (2) don't break any existing functionality. They serve as helpful documentation of how the code is intended to work. In the long term, they tend to shorten development time because they reduce the amount of debugging and testing issues. The current code base has a limited number of these tests but could be significantly improved.\r\n### Detailed Steps\r\nUse JetBrains dotCover to determine the test coverage percentage for the .NET components\r\nUse JetBrains WebStorm to determine the test coverage percentage for the Web front-end\r\nAdd unit tests as needed, prioritizing more business-critical sections of the code and those with complex business or UI logic\r\nIncorporate automated testing into the build pipelines\r\n### Success Metrics\r\n#### Baseline\r\ncurrent test coverage\r\n#### Expected\r\nTest coverage at least 70%, with non-covered sections justified\r\nAutomated unit tests are run as part of the build pipeline; all tests must pass before a build can be released\r\n\r\n## Improve monitoring and telemetry\r\n### Why Recommended\r\nCurrently, the first indication of a problem is usually application slowness or unavailability. Rebooting the servers often resolves the problem, but causes downtime and potential data loss. When rebooting doesn't solve the problem, engaging development can be time-consuming and clunky, relying on passing text-based log files via online file shares or email. More advanced monitoring and telemetry can make it easier to identify problems that have cause—or might soon cause—a loss of service.\r\n### Detailed Steps\r\nStandardize logging\r\nEvaluate shipping logs to S3\r\nIncorporate OpenTelemetry into .NET applications\r\nIdentify and implement a monitoring solution\r\nAdd a '/health' endpoint to each service\r\nFigure out a telemetry collection and alerting strategy (something with OpenTelemetryCollector?)\r\n\r\nNinjaOne? (tickets or health updates)\r\n### Success Metrics\r\n\r\n## Document support details\r\n### Why Recommended\r\nTex Tech's daily operations rely heavily on TTDS, yet the core support team for the application is a single internal IT analyst and a single external developer. If the application should experience an outage and either or both of these individuals were unavailable for an extended time, the business impact could potentially be serious. It would be beneficial to document application components, features, possible points of failure, and troubleshooting steps so that other internal Tex Tech personnel could do first-line support. In the unlikely event that the developer was no longer available, this documentation, along with the source code, would enable a new developer to take over application support.\r\n### Detailed Steps\r\nCreate a high-level architecture diagram, showing all core components and their interactions and dependencies\r\nCreate more detailed component, process flow, state transition, and sequence diagrams to document critical functions\r\nCreate a list of likely failure points and steps to troubleshoot them\r\n### Success Metrics\r\nTex Tech technical personnel unfamiliar with TTDS review and approve the documentation\r\n\r\n## Modernize and streamline sections of the application that make intensive use of the jQuery library\r\n### Why Recommended\r\nWhen HighlandReporting was created in 2017, the state of the art for .NET development was ASP.NET MVC using Razor pages for server-side text templating and jQuery for enhanced client-side interactivity. jQuery has been largely superseded by improvements to the JavaScript language and by improved client-side tools such as React. jQuery is still fully functional and will remain so, but newer developers will be less likely to be familiar with it. Also, the newer tools are generally much faster to work with and are better at managing complex application behavior.\r\n### Detailed Steps\r\nIdentify the application features that could most benefit from modernization\r\nDevelop functional front-end tests using Selenium or similar tooling to ensure baseline functionality for those features\r\nConfirm with Tex Tech that no changes are desired for those features; if changes are desired, adjust the tests to reflect those changes\r\nMigrate the identified features either to React if practical, or to a more modern module-based JavaScript paradigm if moving to React would be too initially complex\r\nRun the tests to ensure 100% behavioral parity\r\n### Success Metrics\r\nTop 5 features are migrated to a newer JavaScript tool set\r\n\r\n\r\nFailure mode\r\nUnit tests\r\n	Recommendations for software quality, performance, and maintainability	2025-11-06 19:46:48.373+00	2025-11-06 19:46:48.373+00
