99	45	2025-11-13T21:52:07.210Z	text/markdown	This is based on lessons learned from the "Removing Interventions" project, which involved a lot of detailed work with Stellar Docs trying to get it set up in a functional test environment.\r\n\r\nAction items:\r\n1. Complete the Migration to a Web Application Project. I have already completed 60-70% of this work. Finishing it would make all future development, testing, and deployment much safer and more predictable.\r\n1. Upgrade jQuery & UI Controls This is the most pressing security concern. Upgrading may require replacing some UI controls, but it also provides a good opportunity to refactor or remove related unused code.\r\n1. Standardize the Data Access Model This is less difficult than it sounds. My recommendation would be to mostly keep the existing stored procs, but gradually migrate the simpler operations to parameterized SQL (which offers similar security and performance benefits to stored procedures but keeps logic in the application code).\r\n1. Refactor and Prune the Codebase This doesn't mean a full rewrite. It means selectively and progressively correcting issues as they're found, such as addressing suppressed errors and removing dead code to make troubleshooting easier.	Action items for project improvements and modernization	2025-11-13 21:54:01.236+00	2025-11-13 21:54:01.236+00
100	45	2025-11-14T20:43:51.200Z	text/markdown	***Here's my email to Holly with the guesstimates she asked for***\r\n\r\n\r\nMigrating to a Web Application Project is probably 15-20 hours. I migrated 17 of the 50 components (pages, controls, etc.) when I was setting up the test system. That includes most of the core components and all but one of the largest ones. And now that I’ve figured out the pattern for how to do it efficiently, it doesn’t take very long to do an individual component.\r\n\r\nHaving that done will make everything else easier.\r\n\r\nI would recommend the “pruning” step next. The nice thing about that is that we can timebox it: whatever pruning we’re able to do will help make things better. My suggestion would be maybe a 15=20 hour timebox and then reassess how much further to go with it. I really don’t know for sure how much of that work there is, I just know that when I was working in there, I kept bumping into things that looked like they weren’t being used anymore.\r\n\r\nThe very next thing should probably be the jQuery and jQuery UI work, because that’s a security risk. Based on what I’m seeing now, that’s probably 30-40 hours (could be less if we end up pruning out a lot of code). Since jQuery UI is no longer actively maintained, the goal will be to eliminate it. We use jQuery UI for modal dialogs and date pickers. The modal dialogs are easy to replace. The date pickers are a lot more complicated. There are a lot of them, and they’re all a little different, then there are server-side calendar controls as well, and we have to make sure everything still works together. \r\n\r\nThe data access work is hard even to guesstimate. Some of it I’ll end up doing just as part of migrating to WAP, and probably some of it will be eliminated with the pruning. Maybe we could timebox that as well, see what we can get done in say 10-15 hours and how much is left to do, and then make sure we still feel like it’s worthwhile.\r\n\r\nIt’s probably worthwhile to add in another 10 hours for testing. So all together we’re talking about 80-100 hours of work here to address the immediate security risks and make the application safer and easier to maintain. I’m ready to work on that wherever it fits into your priorities.	Email with guesstimates for development tasks and security fixes	2025-11-14 20:44:16.956+00	2025-11-14 20:44:16.956+00
