143	192	2026-03-26T16:57:11.370Z	text/markdown	***Summary of onsite visit***\r\n\r\n## Goal: TTDS fully migrated\r\n\r\nThis was completed, but not as smoothly as we had hoped.\r\n\r\nThe quiesce and backup the old server happened very quickly, but then the restore onto the new server just hung. All efforts to kill it failed. In the end, we had to abandon the new database we had started to build and just create a different one on the same server.\r\n\r\nI mistakenly thought we were already running on production from the new servers. In actuality, we had installed TTDS on the new servers but were not running there. In order to get the new servers running correctly, we had to manually install the latest version of TTDS (the HighlandReporting component) on those servers. There were a couple of minor issues with the newest version that had to be patched. Ultimately the planned 2-hour downtime ended up being 3+, with some issues remaining through the rest of the day.\r\n\r\nThe new work order search method, which had worked consistently well over literally months of testing, proved to be extremely slow in production. The reasons aren't entirely clear, but the performance analysis data showed a couple of cases where the database engine was choosing an incorrect execution path. We implemented a couple of additional database artifacts to nudge the engine in the right direction. That helped with some queries, but others were still very slow simply because of data volume. The data showed that nearly 100% of the execution time was spent building the roll status information displayed on the right of the screen. We don't know whether that information is actually useful to anybody. As a stopgap measure, I disabled the parts of the search method that were building those values. Now even large searches come back almost instantly. If it turns out that having that information on the search screen is useful, we'll need to add some additional intermediate database schema items (views, filtered indexes, reporting tables, etc.) to pre-summarize and pre-calculate some of the results.\r\n\r\n## Goal: Spectra Court yardage clock talking to TTDS consistently\r\n\r\nWe made some progress on this, but ran into a major obstacle.\r\n\r\nThe reading process is:\r\n\r\n1. TTDS client in web browser opens web socket connection to TTDS app server, subscribed to a specific device ID\r\n2. clock reader service sends a reading request to the clock via serial connection once per second\r\n3. clock responds with current reading\r\n4. clock reader service posts reading (`deviceID:yardage`) to intake queue for each TTDS app server (only if it's changed or a timeout has elapsed)\r\n5. TTDS retrieves reading from intake queue, parses out device ID and yardage\r\n6. TTDS sends yardage via web socket connection to TTDS clients subscribed to the device ID of the reading\r\n\r\nSteps 1, 5, and 6 are working now. We can push readings directly into the queues and the screen updates.\r\n\r\nStep 4 would almost certainly work if Steps 2 and 3 were working. The problem is the clock communication.\r\n\r\nThe clock reader service uses the .NET serial library to communicate with the clock reader via the COM port. From a programming point of view, it treats it like reading and writing to a file. But it's very different from file I/O. In reality, we've got:\r\n\r\n1. the clock communicating over a 20 mA serial current loop\r\n2. an adapter—appears to be hardwired, very possibly from the clock manufacturer—that converts the 20 mA current loop to RS232 with a DB25 connector\r\n3. a dongle that converts the RS232 signal to USB\r\n4. device mapping inside Windows that maps the USB to a COM port\r\n5. a .NET application that performs file I/O against that COM port\r\n\r\nThere are a lot of pieces there, any of which, if they were even slightly glitchy, could throw the whole thing off. GVIS, by contrast, used (1) a DB25 connector directly on the laptop (no dongle) and (2) a lower-level interface to the COM port, interacting directly with the Win32 API. Either or both of those differences could explain why GVIS mostly worked but the clock reader service so far has not worked consistently.\r\n\r\nI see two primary options for where to go from here:\r\n\r\nA. Modify the clock reader service to use the Win32 API, basically doing exactly what GVIS did\r\nB. Fabricate a simple Raspberry Pi device with a DB25 "hat" and connect the yardage clock to that instead\r\n\r\nOption A is simpler but we still have the USB dongle and a more complex serial connection, so we could do it and still find that it doesn't work.\r\n\r\nOption B is a bigger change, but it's also a much simpler solution to the problem. The actual code would be relatively simple and easy to write. We might need to do a little trial and error, but I'm confident it could be made to work.\r\n\r\n## Goal: quarterly archive process running successfully\r\n\r\nWe never really got to this, but I think we're close to deciding it may not be needed.\r\n\r\n## Goal: we have a clear plan for migrating emissions off Access\r\n\r\nI think we do. We've come up with two enhancements:\r\n\r\n1. Add more readings to the Toluene Readings page\r\n2. Add an "attach SDS" feature to the Coating Component page\r\n\r\nIf I'm understanding correctly, though, neither of these were really blockers. I think all the pieces are there and it's just a matter of finding time to actually start using the system. I'm happy to support that process any way I can.\r\n\r\nThanks again for letting me come out. It was nice to see Charles, Joe, and Emma again, and nice to finally meet Mark, Jerry, and Alex in person.	Summary of onsite visit	2026-03-26 17:47:53.246013+00	2026-03-26 17:47:53.246013+00
