196	228	2026-06-01T21:33:10.741Z	text/markdown	Turns out this didn't work because I never got as far as adding it.\r\n\r\nI've put in a temporary kludge to just reload the page when a SignalR event fires, but that's super annoying.\r\n\r\nI don't love the idea of implementing the conditional formatting logic twice, but the alternative is to use partials. But then I'd have to decide whether the partial should be a cell, a row, or the whole `tbody`. Doing the whole `tbody` has the potential of creating the same scrolling issues. To do cells or rows, we'd have to keep track of what's changed. So my instinct is just to keep the server-side conditional formatting on page load, to avoid any weirdness with loading delays, and then to have completely separate JavaScript code that does the same conditional formatting—both the red/green + icon and the scrapped rows—on an affected-cell basis in response to the SignalR trigger. That's the next item on the list.	Planning client-side conditional formatting for SignalR events	2026-06-01 21:36:55.007565+00	2026-06-01 21:36:55.007565+00
